Take the Linux console's bell sequences and report the tone they set

The Linux console sets its bell's pitch with CSI 10 ; n ], in Hz, and its
length with CSI 11 ; n ], in milliseconds, which setterm writes; a length
of 2000 or more is 0, which silences the bell, and either sequence with no
n sets the default again. do_csi's ] case took only CSI 16 ; n ].

Keep both values, -1 for the default, which a reset sets again, and add
tsm_vte_get_bell_tone() to read them, so that the program that sounds the
bell from the bell callback can sound it at the pitch and length asked
for. The default is the caller's to choose. A ] sequence with a private
marker is ignored.

--- a/src/tsm/libtsm.h
+++ b/src/tsm/libtsm.h
@@ -495,6 +495,21 @@
 void tsm_vte_set_led_cb(struct tsm_vte *vte, tsm_vte_led_cb led_cb, void *led_data);
 
 /**
+ * @brief Read the bell's tone that the Linux console's sequences set.
+ *
+ * CSI 10 ; n ] sets the bell's pitch to n Hz and CSI 11 ; n ] its length to
+ * n milliseconds, where 2000 or more is 0, silent, as Linux's console takes
+ * them. Either with no n, and a reset, return it to the default, which is
+ * the caller's to choose. Sounding the bell is the caller's too, from the
+ * bell callback.
+ *
+ * @param vte The vte object
+ * @param hz Where the pitch in Hz is stored, or -1 for the default
+ * @param ms Where the length in milliseconds is stored, or -1 for the default
+ */
+void tsm_vte_get_bell_tone(struct tsm_vte *vte, int *hz, int *ms);
+
+/**
  * @brief Set color palette to one of the predefined palette on the vte object.
  *
  * Current supported palette names are
